          "path": "_m/r/c/f/u/cape-reinga-wp/leptospermum-scoparium-1020094.jpg",
          "filename": "Leptospermum scoparium (10200946605).jpg",
          "caption": "Mānuka (Leptospermum scoparium) growing at Cape Reinga.\nCape Reinga is generally considered the separation marker between the Tasman Sea to the west and the Pacific Ocean to the east. From the lighthouse it is possible to watch the tidal race, as the two seas clash to create unsettled waters just off the coast. The Māori refer to this as the meeting of Te Moana-a-Rehua, 'the sea of Rehua' with Te Tai-o-Whitireia, 'the sea of Whitireia', Rehua and Whitireia being a male and a female respectively. - 07 October 2013\n\nsource: en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cape_Reinga",
          "alt": "Mānuka (Leptospermum scoparium) growing at Cape Reinga.\nCape Reinga is generally considered the separation marker between the Tasman Sea to the west and the Pacific Ocean to the east. From the lighthouse it is possible to watch the tidal race, as the two seas clash to create unsettled waters just off the coast. The Māori refer to this as the meeting of Te Moana-a-Rehua, 'the sea of Rehua' with Te Tai-o-Whitireia, 'the sea of Whitireia', Rehua and Whitireia being a male and a female respectively. - 07 October 2013\n\nsource: en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cape_Reinga",
          "width": 1600,
          "height": 1067,
          "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
          "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
